LOWRY VETOES PORN BILL 5/13/95

JONATHAN MARTIN, SEATTLE TIMES OLYMPIA BUREAU

OLYMPIA - Gov. Mike Lowry's veto yesterday of a controversial bill  designed
to shelter minors from pornography has prompted Democrats and Republicans to
debate how - not if - the veto should be nullified.

Supporters of the bill now have two options: attempting a veto override, or
reintroducing the bill in order to try to reach a compromise with Lowry.

Most Republicans, including Senate Minority Leader Dan McDonald and House
Speaker Clyde Ballard, want an override and believe they have enough support
to get the necessary approval of two-thirds of each house.

The bill, SB5466, was approved by 119 of the state's 147 lawmakers.

But an override must originate in the Senate, which is controlled by
Democrats, and Senate Majority Leader Marc Gaspard does not favor a direct
challenge to fellow Democrat Lowry. He plans to reintroduce the bill on
Monday and start negotiating with the governor again.

"That's the method we'll use to resolve this," said Gaspard.

The bill would criminalize the act of displaying or selling magazines,
books, theater or movie tickets, rec ords or online access to minors if the
action allowed them to see pornographic material. No such working law is on
the state books.

Sen. Adam Smith, D-Kent, has sponsored the bill for two consecutive years in
an attempt to go after pedophiles and child molesters who use pornography to
entice children. Last year, after Lowry vetoed a nearly identical proposal,
Smith worked to craft a compromise that would be acceptable to Lowry.

Smith said he will meet on Monday with Republicans and representatives of the
governor's office. "The only thing I see is that I want to get a bill into
law," said Smith, a Seattle assistant city attorney.

Lowry said in his veto message that the bill would result in censorship when
merchants or artists - "especially those . . . at the fore of technology,
medicine or artistic movement" - eliminate questionable material in order to
reduce their potential liability.

"The government intruding into censorship has caused some of the worst
man-made disasters in history," Lowry said in a recent interview.

He supports changes to state laws prohibiting adults from luring children
into sexually abusive situations. But he believes the bills he vetoed
yesterday and last year violate free speech protections.

To determine what is pornographic, the bill uses a three-pronged, U.S.
Supreme Court definition called the Miller Test. At least 30 states have
"harmful-to-minors" statutes that have been upheld in state Supreme Courts.
Stewart Jay, a University of Washington professor of constitutional law, said
the bill likely would be thrown out by the state Supreme Court because it
violates two principles used in anti-pornography legislation.

