Subject: Limiting newsgroups: Banning or providing?
Date: 13 May 1995 16:26:32 +1000
Message-ID: <3p1jeo$b0j@calvin.abc.gov.au>

[uk.net and bham.usenet.misc trimmed from followups.]

John McGinlay <John@mcgwin.demon.co.uk> writes:

>   No University has 'banned' any group.
>   A more realistic line should be
>   UK University Provides Usenet NewsGroups
>   on the proviso that they are relevent to the business of the university.

 I do not wish to argue the policy decisions of any news-providing
body, be it university or private, but I would like to raise a point
regarding the "bans"/"provides" issue mentioned above.

 If the truth be known, the whole of Usenet is not available at most
news-sites. The alt group is the most frequently ignored, and this
often affects sites downstream that would otherwise have liked to
carry such groups. That aside, certain organisations, private and
public, will explicitly select to carry only a subset of groups
available to them. I see the choice as being a simple matter of
_exclusion_ and/or _inclusion._

 To illustrate: a BBS may wish to carry a subset of Usenet as a
service to its members. Let us assume that resources are already
somewhat limited for this BBS, and because they cater mainly to
Macintosh users, they carry only the comp.mac.* groups. This is a case
of explicit inclusion: starting from no groups {exclude *}, they then
explicitly include the mac groups {include comp.mac.*}. The two set
operators that I have used not only reflect the actual process used to
obtain the groups, but give a clear indication of the rationale behind
the process.

 Compare that to the following situation: a University decides to
exclude the alt.sex.* groups for political or potential legal reasons.
Their news server is then set up to {include *; exclude alt.sex.*}.

 I suggest that there is a qualatative difference between these two
approaches (despite the result being "a subset of Usenet" in both
cases). To draw an analogy: if Usenet were a piece of cloth, the BBS
would provide you with a handkerchief and the university would provide
you with a sheet that has some neat holes cut in it.

 I do not wish to accuse the University in question (or anyone else)
of censorship - I consider it a misuse of the word. My point is that I
*do* consider it fair and correct to say that the university in
question has *banned* certain newsgroups as opposed to saying it
*provides* certain newsgroups. The reverse applies for the BBS: it
*provides* certain groups. If "banned" is too emotive a term, I suggest
using "excluded" instead, but to describe an instance of "banning" as
"providing" is (IMHO) at minimum giving the wrong impression.
