Let's be straight.  I'm not disagreeing with your basic idea.  I was
attempting to give one instance of a case in which a librarian may
have some difficulty with unrestricted access.  I.e. k-12.  For
purposes of discussion, I'll accept the application of ALA policy to
net access.

Let me try an example:
1. Primary school library is providing a full news feed.
2. Students discover the wonderful world of alt.sex.stories
3. Parent X finds out.
4. X comes screaming into the library, demanding the removal of all
   this "smut".
5. Librarian calmly explains the library's role and explains that if
   any further action is to be taken, X must submit a formal written
   complaint according to the "reconsideration" section of their
   selection policy (which has followed all recommendations in the
   ALA's Workbook for Selection Policy Writing.)
6. A review committee is assigned to examine a.s.s (as per this policy)
7. The committee reports to the school board (as per this policy) that
   a.s.s is not supported by the stated selection criteria
   (e.g. little educational significance, no favorable reviews found
   in standard selection sources, little "validity, currency, and
   appropriates of material", little "artistic quality and/or literary
   style", etc.)  Actually, the report would more likely read: "What
   are we doing with this filth in the library and how much more is
   there?"
8. a.s.s is removed along with a slew of others at the direction of
   the chief administrator after evaluation by the above committee.
9. The a.s hierarchy is reduced to a shadow of its former glory,
   all in accordance with ALA policies.

This is not surprising.  I don't remember Penthouse or Playboy on the
stacks next to OWL (a "kids" mag) when I was a kid, even if some joker
left a copy lying around.  They exist here at the university (on
microfilm).  You are fighting an uphill battle if you think this
should change.  (just inserted as a sanity check.  You're supposed to
start saying ...no,no ... strawman ... putting words in my mouth,
etc. here to reassure me that we have some common ground.)


The ALA recognises that not all material is suitable in all
circumstances.

From the above mentioned handbook:
          "Learning resources shall be appropriate for the
          subject area and for the age, emotional
          development, ability level, learning styles and
          social development of the students for whom the
          materials are selected."

And another quote (one of my favorites) from "THE FREEDOM TO READ" reads:
   "There is no place in our society for efforts to coerce the
    taste of others, to confine adults to the reading matter
    deemed suitable for adolescents, or to inhibit the efforts
    of writers to achieve artistic expression."

While I'm stretching here, I think the usage of the word 'adults'
implies that the ALA is willing to accept that not all material is
suitable for "adolescents".  They actually state this but place the
responsibility solely on parents to control their (and only their)
children's access to material.

Now, we throw in the www.

The above process breaks down at (what I see as) a very important
step:
WWW access is not, in any meaningful sense, selected.  I mean that,
unless you take the unlikely tack of describing the whole shebang as a
single work, there is little or no mechanism for selecting parts as
well suited to the educational mission of the school (or more likely
un-selecting material as particularly unsuited to the purpose).  This
lack of control makes a selection policy difficult to implement.

This means that the above story has a confusing ending.  Assuming X's
child finds such questionable material on the web, do we:
1. do nothing
2. put that specific url in some "kill file" type thingie
3. kill all web access

I don't think (1) is an option.  The ALA explicitly allows for the
removal of material in a school situation after careful review.
Parents (and newspapers, administrators, etc.) certainly won't put up
with it.

(2) is difficult.  There is no organization (unlike usenet :). Given
the size of the web, it would take a full time censor just to wade
through part of it.  

(3) is a likely outcome.

Simply striking offending pages as parents complain (after review by
committee) seems most in line with stated ALA policy, but with the
www, this would be very cumbersome.  WWW is very open and free form.

To (finally) get to the point, and give you some questions to answer:
Do you see a distinction between 'traditional' media and net access
with respect to their selectablilty?
If not, why not?
If so, why do you believe this difference to be irrelevant to the
application of ALA policy in the special case of a k-12 library/"media
centre"?

I am hoping you see my distinction as important or at least can
explain why it isn't.

As my mind is not yet made up on some substantial issues, I am quite
interested in this discussion.

