I agree that the K-12 Web access is different than K-12 newsgroup and
book selection, but I do see some useful analogies to traditional
media:

   Web access is like
          Interlibrary loan requests from a K-12 library to a public library.
          Getting a k-12er getting a library card for a university
                library.
          Taking kids on a field trip to a public museum.

In the later two cases, explicit parental permission is usually
required. But if it is given, the library and museum will generally
not act in place of the parents. Likewise in the first case, the
public library will generally not act in place of the parents.

But what about the teacher or K-12 librarian monitoring and
restricting access? I don't know.

What about services that now offer restricted Net access. I think it
is fine for parents to sign up for such a service. But schools should
not sign up for such services if the service bases its selection
policy on politics not educational value.

How about this policy:

    On-site resources are to be selected like text books or library
       material.

    Any access to off-site resources requires parental permission.

    Full access will be the default, but parents may request that
       their (and only their) children's access be restricted to any
       particular service if they are willing to pay the cost of
       restricted service.

- Carl
