From: ccpes@sun.cse.bris.ac.uk (Paul Smee)
Subject: Re: UK University bans newsgroups.
Message-ID: <D8u656.LsH@info.bris.ac.uk>
Date: Fri, 19 May 1995 17:34:17 GMT

In article <3pi91n$st0@decaxp.harvard.edu>,
David Horne <horne@fas.harvard.edu> wrote:
>I was wondering how many of the UK universities carry the various
>alt.binaries.pictures.erotica newsgroups? 

Most don't.  The people who run the (government-funded) academic
network have made it a condition of connecting to that network that it
may not be used for transporting 'unapproved' newsgroups - which, in
essence, excludes alt.binaries.pictures.erotica.*, alt.drugs.* (or
whatever it's called) and alt.sex.*.

There's nothing to stop Unis who are so inclined from paying for
commercial connections to providers who *will* provide such groups, and
I am believe some do, as long as they don't then redistribute them out
through the publicly funded net.

As you might imagine, this is an area of continual argument, and I, for
one, think it would be fun if some department could (and would) try to
make a case for these on straightforward and honest academic grounds -
not vague 'we should carry them' arguments, but direct 'this is our
area of research and we need them' grounds.

(Of course, when considering 'freedom of information' in the UK, you
need to remember that it's been mere days since the Prime Minister made
a statement to the effect that 'open government requires that
democratically elected representatives should not be required to tell
the public what companies and lobbying groups they receive money from',
so...)

